   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "Don&#39;t cast the result of <code>malloc</code> to <code>(int*)</code>; the cast will happen automatically. DRY."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 21767546,
   "parent_id": 21767320,
   "author_user_id": 65863,
   "author_display_name": "Remy Lebeau",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/21767546",
   "score": 3,
   "created_utc": "2014-02-13T23:08:46Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2014-02-13T23:08:46Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>Technically, it is correct.  However, this is C++ we are talking about, and the C++ way to dynamically allocate an array is to use a <code>std:vector</code> instead:</p>\n\n<pre><code>std::vector&lt;int&gt; Image(m_Width/2 * m_Height);\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Or:</p>\n\n<pre><code>std::vector&lt;int&gt; Image;\nImage.resize(m_Width/2 * m_Height);\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>The memory will be freed automatically when the <code>std::vector</code> is destructed when it goes out of scope.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 32929468,
   "parent_id": 21767320,
   "parent_type": "question",
   "author_user_id": 1577580,
