   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "Sorry for any wrong info. Is the behavior different in C than C++? <a href=\"http://stackoverflow.com/questions/605845/do-i-cast-the-result-of-malloc\">This</a> is what I was going by. I can&#39;t believe how rusty I&#39;ve gotten after working in C#."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 32930481,
   "parent_id": 21767546,
   "parent_type": "answer",
   "author_user_id": 65863,
   "author_display_name": "Remy Lebeau",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/21767320/#comment32930481_21767546",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2014-02-13T23:46:49Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "The original code was allocating an array of <code>int</code> values, so you would create a <code>std::vector</code> of <code>int</code> values."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 21774252,
   "parent_id": 21767320,
   "author_user_id": 3308710,
   "author_display_name": "Migun",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/21774252",
   "score": 2,
   "created_utc": "2014-02-14T08:27:37Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2014-02-14T08:27:37Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>That's correct. But if you want to gain some additional semantics without getting much OOP overhead, you can use unique_ptr:</p>\n\n<pre><code>unique_ptr&lt;int[]&gt; Image(new int[m_Width/2 * m_Height]);\n// do something with Image...\nImage.Reset(); // call it if you want to free memory manually,\n               // or just leave until Image is destroyed. \n</code></pre>\n"
  },
